# Copy to .env and fill in real values. Never commit the filled-in .env.

NODE_ENV=development
PORT=8080

# Prisma / Postgres
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/DATABASE?schema=public"

# Database TLS certificate (Aiven MySQL)
#
# Prisma verifies the server certificate against this file and reads it from the
# connection URL, not from the schema datasource block and not from
# prisma.config.ts — neither of those accepts one. Leave it unset for a local
# MySQL without TLS.
#
# When it is set, the path is resolved to an absolute one (a relative path is
# read from backend/, not from the directory Prisma happens to run in), the file
# has to exist and be readable, and Prisma is told to verify the certificate
# rather than accept any certificate. A wrong path then fails at startup with
# this variable named, instead of Prisma's "Can't reach database server".
#
#   Development, with the CA in the git-ignored certificates/ folder:
#     DB_CA_CERT_PATH=./certificates/aiven-ca.pem
#   Production, as a Render Secret File mounted outside the repository:
#     DB_CA_CERT_PATH=/etc/secrets/aiven-ca.pem
#
# Safer still in production: leave DB_CA_CERT_PATH unset. At startup the app
# looks for the certificate under /etc/secrets/ on its own — matching the
# configured basename, then aunique certificate-looking secret file — so
# creating the Render Secret File is all that is required. A dev value that was
# copied to Render ("./certificates/...") is resolved the same way and also
# finds the mounted secret instead of failing.
# This covers the running application only. `prisma migrate` and the other CLI
# commands build their own connection from DATABASE_URL, so a migration run
# against Aiven needs the certificate written into DATABASE_URL itself.
# DB_CA_CERT_PATH=
#
# When the database is remote, add the pool settings to DATABASE_URL as well:
#   ...?sslcert=/etc/secrets/aiven-ca.pem&sslaccept=strict&connection_limit=3&pool_timeout=30&connect_timeout=20
# A remote database over TLS pays a handshake for every pooled connection, and
# the defaults (5 connections, a 10s wait for a free one, 5s to connect) are tight
# enough that a busy start can fail a login with "Timed out fetching a new
# connection from the connection pool". Fewer connections means fewer
# simultaneous handshakes; the longer timeouts mean a request waits instead of
# failing. Raise connection_limit for a busier deployment.

# Redis — BullMQ queues and caching.
# Local (WSL 2 Ubuntu Redis): redis://127.0.0.1:6379
# Production (Render etc.):    redis://<user>:<password>@<host>:6379
# Omit to fall back to redis://127.0.0.1:6379
REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379

# Rate limiting — Redis-backed, so the counters are shared by every instance.
# When Redis is unreachable the same limits are applied from an in-process
# in-memory store instead: requests keep being limited, just per process rather
# than per cluster. Nothing has to be restarted or reconfigured for that to
# happen, and Redis mode resumes by itself once the server is back.
# Each value is "requests per window". Optional: the defaults below apply when a
# line is absent or commented out.
#   RATE_LIMIT_API_MAX/WINDOW_MS             whole API, health probe excluded
#   RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX/WINDOW_MS            every /api/auth endpoint together
#   RATE_LIMIT_PASSWORD_RESET_MAX/WINDOW_MS  forgot/verify/reset-password only
#   RATE_LIMIT_AI_MAX/WINDOW_MS              /api/ai
#   RATE_LIMIT_UPLOAD_MAX/WINDOW_MS          any multipart request
# RATE_LIMIT_API_MAX=100
# RATE_LIMIT_API_WINDOW_MS=60000
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX=10
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_WINDOW_MS=60000
# RATE_LIMIT_PASSWORD_RESET_MAX=5
# RATE_LIMIT_PASSWORD_RESET_WINDOW_MS=900000
# RATE_LIMIT_AI_MAX=20
# RATE_LIMIT_AI_WINDOW_MS=60000
# RATE_LIMIT_UPLOAD_MAX=30
# RATE_LIMIT_UPLOAD_WINDOW_MS=60000
# While Redis is down, one request per window is used to test whether it is back.
# This is how long that window is, in ms — raise it if the log line about
# reconnecting is more frequent than you want to read.
# RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_PROBE_MS=5000
# The Redis client in config/redis.js stops retrying after a while and does not
# reconnect on its own. This is how long the limiter waits between asking that
# same client to try again, in ms.
# RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_RECONNECT_MS=30000

# Auth — generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('hex'))"
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=

# Frontend origin allowed by CORS
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# Auth cookie SameSite policy. Defaults to "none" when NODE_ENV=production
# (the SPA and API live on different sites, e.g. localhost against Render, and
# "strict" would silently drop the cookies — "Session expired" on every call).
# Set to strict|lax when both sides share one site.
#COOKIE_SAMESITE=

# Google Sign-In ("Continue with Google").
#
# Create an OAuth 2.0 Web application client at:
#   https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
# then paste its credentials here. The values below are placeholders and are
# treated as NOT configured, so a fresh clone disables the Google button instead
# of sending a real authorization request carrying "your_client_id_here".
#
# Authorized redirect URI in Google Cloud must match GOOGLE_CALLBACK_URL exactly,
# and Authorized JavaScript origin must be your frontend origin (the same value
# as CORS_ORIGIN above):
#   http://localhost:5173
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET is backend-only. Never put it in frontend/.env, and never
# prefix it with VITE_ — Vite ships every VITE_ variable to the browser.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID is public by design (it appears in the authorization URL) but
# this flow does not need it in the frontend, so it stays here.
#
# The frontend is sent back to CORS_ORIGIN after the callback. Set
# GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URL only if the landing page differs from the origin.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_client_id_here
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your_client_secret_here
GOOGLE_CALLBACK_URL=http://localhost:8080/api/auth/google/callback

# GitHub Sign-In ("Continue with GitHub").
#
# Create an OAuth App at:
#   GitHub > Settings > Developer settings > OAuth Apps > New OAuth app
# and paste its credentials here. As above, these placeholders are treated as
# NOT configured, so a fresh clone answers "GitHub is not available yet" instead
# of sending a real authorization request carrying "your_client_id_here".
#
# Exact values to enter in that form, locally:
#   Application name          Taskora (or anything you recognise)
#   Homepage URL              http://localhost:5173
#   Authorization callback URL
#                             http://localhost:8080/api/auth/github/callback
#
# The callback URL must match GITHUB_CALLBACK_URL exactly — scheme, host, port
# and path, one app per URL. An OAuth App gives you a Client ID and (after you
# press "Generate a new client secret") a Client Secret.
#
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET is backend-only. Never put it in frontend/.env and never
# prefix it with VITE_ — Vite ships every VITE_ variable to the browser.
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID is public by design (it appears in the authorization URL);
# this flow does not need it in the frontend, so it stays here.
#
# For a deployed instance, register a second OAuth App whose callback URL is
# https://<your-backend-host>/api/auth/github/callback and set its values on
# that environment. The frontend is sent back to CORS_ORIGIN after the callback;
# set GITHUB_REDIRECT_URL only if the landing page differs from the origin.
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=your_client_id_here
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=your_client_secret_here
GITHUB_CALLBACK_URL=http://localhost:8080/api/auth/github/callback

# Fallback IANA timezone for date/time-aware AI replies, used only when a user
# has no timezone set on their profile. Per-user settings take precedence.
APP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Manila

# Uploads
UPLOAD_DIR=uploads
MAX_FILE_SIZE_BYTES=5242880

# AI providers — at least one is required for the AI endpoints
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.8-flash
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_MODEL=openrouter/auto
# Upper bound on a single AI reply, in tokens. Raise it if replies are being cut
# off before they finish a sentence.
AI_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8192

# Text to speech
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# Email (Brevo) — transactional mail for password reset codes, the signed-in
# change-password code, and notification email. Both BREVO_API_KEY and
# BREVO_SENDER_EMAIL are required or every send fails with 503. The sender must
# be a verified sender/domain in the Brevo panel. Never expose these to the
# frontend.
BREVO_API_KEY=
BREVO_SENDER_EMAIL=
BREVO_SENDER_NAME=Taskora

# Daily summary — Emails a recap of your day.
# Existing code runs an hourly-aligned scan (DAILY_SUMMARY_CHECK_INTERVAL_MS,
# default 30 minutes) that queues one recap per eligible account whose *local*
# timezone has reached DAILY_SUMMARY_HOUR (24-hour hour, default 8 = 8:00 AM).
# A user is eligible only when both the master email switch and the Daily
# summary switch are on, and the recap is sent once per local calendar day.
# DAILY_SUMMARY_HOUR=8
# DAILY_SUMMARY_CHECK_INTERVAL_MS=1800000

# Web Push — browser push for push notifications. All three are required or the
# feature reports itself as disabled in Settings; a missing key degrades to "no
# push", never to a broken Settings page.
#   VAPID_SUBJECT   a mailto:/https contact, e.g. mailto:you@example.com
#   VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY / VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY   generate once with:
#     node -e "console.log(JSON.stringify(require('web-push').generateVAPIDKeys(), null, 2))"
# The public key is public (the browser needs it to subscribe); the private key
# must never leave the backend.
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Email validation (ZeroBounce) — deliverability check on new signups.
# Runs only when NODE_ENV=production. In development and test the check is
# skipped entirely and no request is made, even if a key is present.
# In production ZEROBOUNCE_API_KEY is required: a missing key, an upstream
# error, a timeout, or an exhausted credit balance fails registration with 503
# rather than admitting unchecked addresses. Never expose this to the frontend.
ZEROBOUNCE_API_KEY=
# Optional endpoint override for regional processing. Defaults to the global
# endpoint; use https://api-eu.zerobounce.net/v2/validate or
# https://api-us.zerobounce.net/v2/validate to pin the data region.
# ZEROBOUNCE_API_URL=https://api.zerobounce.net/v2/validate
